This component (ffmpeg-bundle) packages the FFmpeg static archives
(libavformat, libavcodec, libavutil, libswscale, libswresample) and
exposes them to Rust callers via a thin FFI shim plus a small C
accessor file. Because it statically incorporates the FFmpeg LGPL
libraries, the combined work is distributed under the terms of the
GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or later
(LGPL-2.1-or-later).

The full text of the LGPL-2.1 license is available at:

  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.txt

and is reproduced verbatim in
`sources/ffmpeg-n7.1/COPYING.LGPLv2.1` inside this directory.

What this means for downstream consumers:

  - You may use, modify, and redistribute this crate under the
    LGPL-2.1.
  - If you statically link this crate into another binary, the
    LGPL-2.1's source-availability obligation applies — you must
    provide a way for recipients of the binary to obtain the
    corresponding FFmpeg source. The FFmpeg source we build against
    is already vendored at `sources/ffmpeg-n7.1/` and the build
    recipe at `scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh` produces the linked
    archives bit-for-bit; satisfying §6 of the LGPL is therefore
    automatic for anyone who has access to this repository.
  - Dynamic linking against the produced static archives is not
    materially different from static linking for licensing purposes
    — both routes preserve LGPL-2.1's terms on the FFmpeg portion.

The Rust FFI shim (`src/lib.rs`, `src/accessors.c`, `build.rs`)
is original code authored as part of this crate and is licensed
under LGPL-2.1-or-later for compositional clarity — though,
considered in isolation, it is short and uninteresting and could
just as easily have been MIT.

